\b MyFps UE5.5 Technical Summary - Multiplayer Combat Damage Sync\b0\par
\par
\b 1. Goal\b0\par
This work focused on making player-versus-player combat function correctly in multiplayer. The target behavior was:\par
- players can damage each other through server-authoritative fire logic;\par
- the correct target takes damage and can die;\par
- kill, death, and score changes are synchronized correctly.\par
\par
\b 2. Core Problem\b0\par
The original combat issue was not team configuration. Unreal's ApplyDamage does not require teams by default. The real problem was that the server-side weapon trace and hit-detection path were not reliably selecting the player character as the hit target.\par
\par
There were two main causes:\par
- the previous damage trace used a general-purpose visibility channel, which mixed weapon, character, and world visibility behavior together;\par
- held weapon actors could interfere with the trace path and cause the server to hit the wrong object or fail to identify the player body cleanly.\par
\par
\b 3. Dedicated Bullet Trace Channel\b0\par
A dedicated trace channel named BulletTrace was introduced at the project collision-profile level. In DefaultEngine.ini it is defined as a custom trace channel using ECC_GameTraceChannel2.\par
\par
This changed the combat model from:\par
- generic Visibility trace,\par
to:\par
- weapon damage trace using BulletTrace only.\par
\par
This separation is important because it decouples bullet hit-detection from unrelated visibility use cases such as interactable highlighting, camera checks, or visual line-of-sight behavior.\par
\par
\b 4. Server-Authoritative Aim Trace\b0\par
The weapon component now computes combat hit-detection on the server using view-based trace data. The relevant path is:\par
- get view location and aim rotation;\par
- trace forward from the server-authoritative view direction;\par
- determine the aim point from that trace;\par
- apply damage only on the server.\par
\par
The server trace explicitly ignores:\par
- the firing character;\par
- the weapon actor itself.\par
\par
This prevents the local weapon from blocking the shooter's own damage trace and keeps the hit-selection path focused on the actual target or world geometry.\par
\par
\b 5. Separation of Damage and Visual Tracer\b0\par
The combat system now cleanly separates two concerns:\par
\par
\i Damage logic\i0\par
- decided by the server's BulletTrace result.\par
\par
\i Visual tracer logic\i0\par
- still spawned from the muzzle toward the final aim point so the shot visually leaves the gun.\par
\par
This design allows the shooter to keep believable weapon-origin tracer visuals while the actual damage target is still chosen from a clean server trace that is not distorted by third-person socket or muzzle placement issues.\par
\par
\b 6. Health, Death, and Scoring Chain\b0\par
Once the server trace identifies a valid player target, the following chain executes:\par
- server calls ApplyDamage on the hit actor;\par
- the target player character processes TakeDamage on the authority side;\par
- health is reduced;\par
- when health reaches zero, Die is called;\par
- death state is replicated to clients;\par
- the server registers score, kill, and death statistics through GameMode and PlayerState.\par
\par
Player-vs-player kills now award score through the same authoritative match flow used elsewhere in the project, so reaching the target score can drive the end-of-match condition.\par
\par
\b 7. Resulting Design Principles\b0\par
The multiplayer combat route now follows these principles:\par
- only the server decides damage;\par
- only the server decides who was hit;\par
- traces for combat use a dedicated BulletTrace channel;\par
- the shooter and equipped weapon are excluded from the hit-selection path;\par
- visual tracer feedback is allowed to remain separate from damage calculation.\par
\par
\b 8. Outcome\b0\par
With these changes in place, multiplayer player-versus-player damage became stable again. Players can now hit each other, lose health, die, and feed the score system correctly through a server-authoritative combat pipeline.\par
